  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Sarmentosumin D and Phosphodiesterase 6C, mediated through the CD276 antigen. This relationship is supported by multiple pathways, each indicating that Sarmentosumin D targets the CD276 antigen, which in turn targets Phosphodiesterase 6C. The scores from the three paths, ranging from 0.4752 to 0.4964, indicate a consistent level of confidence in this interaction, with the highest score in path_414193. The shared target of CD276 antigen across all three paths suggests a common mechanism, possibly involving the regulation of phosphodiesterase activity as a downstream effect of CD276 engagement. The evidence highlights the role of CD276 as a central node in the interaction network, with Sarmentosumin D acting as a modulator of its downstream targets. While the exact mechanism of how Sarmentosumin D influences Phosphodiesterase 6C remains to be fully elucidated, the integrated evidence supports a plausible, biologically relevant relationship. The consistency across the three paths, along with the presence of multiple targets of CD276, strengthens the case for this interaction, though further experimental validation would be necessary to confirm its functional significance."
